---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/paths';
import EmergencyNotice from '../components/EmergencyNotice.astro';
import { MAST_CELL_BASIS, MAST_CELL_BASIS_INFO, type MastCellBasis } from '../lib/vocab';

const medications = (await getCollection('medications')).filter((e) => !e.data.draft);
const supplements = (await getCollection('supplements')).filter((e) => !e.data.draft);
const foods = await getCollection('foods');
const resources = await getCollection('resources');

const treatments = [...medications, ...supplements];
const downstream = treatments.filter((e) => e.data.mastCellBasis === 'downstream').length;
const studiedInMcas = treatments.filter((e) => e.data.mastCellBasis === 'mcas-patients').length;
const citationCount = treatments.reduce((n, e) => n + e.data.citations.length, 0);

const SECTIONS = [
  {
    path: '/medications',
    label: 'Medications',
    count: medications.length,
    blurb:
      'Mast cell stabilisers, antihistamines, and kinase inhibitors, each with a sourced mechanism and an explicit evidence tier.',
  },
  {
    path: '/supplements',
    label: 'Supplements',
    count: supplements.length,
    blurb:
      'Held to the same bar as medications. Most of what clears it sits at the mechanistic tier, and the badges say so.',
  },
  {
    path: '/foods',
    label: 'Food & triggers',
    count: foods.length,
    blurb:
      'FODMAP, histamine, oxalate and tyramine ratings shown per source, with the basis for each exclusion recorded rather than assumed.',
  },
  {
    path: '/resources',
    label: 'Resources',
    count: resources.length,
    blurb:
      'Diagnostic criteria, key papers, and patient organizations, each tagged with what it is and when it was last checked.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="What mast cell activation syndrome is, and what is known about treating it"
  description="An index of treatments and triggers discussed in mast cell activation disease, recording the published mechanism for each and how strong the evidence behind it actually is."
>
  <section class="max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight">
      Mast cell activation syndrome, and what is actually known about it
    </h1>
    <p class="prose-body mt-4 text-lg" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <a href={href('/glossary#mast-cell')}>Mast cells</a> are immune cells that sit
      in the skin, gut, airways and around blood vessels. They store
      <a href={href('/glossary#histamine')}>histamine</a> and other chemicals and
      release them when triggered. In mast cell activation syndrome they release
      them too readily, and the result is repeated episodes affecting more than
      one part of the body at once.
    </p>
    <p class="prose-body mt-3" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      This site indexes the treatments and triggers discussed for it. For each
      one it records what the drug or food does, and how strong the evidence
      behind that is. It carries no dosing and never says anything works.
      <a href={href('/glossary')}>Every term used here is defined</a>, and
      <a href={href('/methodology')}>the rules are enforced by the build</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="mt-6 max-w-3xl">
    <EmergencyNotice />
  </div>

  <section class="mt-10 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">Where to start</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">I think I might have this</h3>
        <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
          There is no symptom checklist here, and that is deliberate. These
          symptoms overlap several commoner conditions, so a list would invite a
          conclusion the evidence cannot support. What a diagnosis actually
          requires is a different question, and a more useful one.
        </p>
        <ol class="prose-body mt-3 ml-5 list-decimal space-y-1.5 text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
          <li>
            <a href={href('/advocacy#the-criteria')}>Read what a diagnosis requires</a> —
            three criteria, and two competing versions of them.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={href('/advocacy#the-core-panel')}>Note the timing of the tests</a>.
            The main blood test only means something if it is drawn within a few
            hours of an episode.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={href('/advocacy#finding-a-clinician')}>Find someone to take it to</a>,
            then <a href={href('/appointment')}>build a sheet</a> to bring.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="card p-5">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">I have a diagnosis</h3>
        <p class="prose-body mt-2 text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
          The directories below are the reference material. Two things are worth
          knowing before reading them.
        </p>
        <ul class="prose-body mt-3 ml-5 list-disc space-y-1.5 text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
          <li>
            Nothing is approved for MCAS itself, so every entry borrows its
            evidence from another condition. Each one says which.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={href('/foods')}>Food lists disagree with each other</a>, and
            the directory shows where they conflict rather than hiding it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href={href('/appointment')}>The appointment sheet</a> prints
            selected entries with their mechanisms and citations.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="prose-body mt-3 text-sm" style="color: var(--text-faint);">
      None of this is medical advice, and no page here can tell you whether you
      have this condition.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ul class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    {
      SECTIONS.map((s) => (
        <li class="card p-5">
          <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold">
            <a href={href(s.path)} class="no-underline hover:underline" style="color: var(--text);">
              {s.label}
            </a>
            <span class="ml-2 text-sm font-normal" style="color: var(--text-faint);">
              {s.count}
            </span>
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm" style="color: var(--text-muted);">{s.blurb}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">How to read an entry</h2>
    <p class="prose-body mt-2" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <strong style="color: var(--text);">
        Nothing here is approved for MCAS, and almost nothing has been tested in it.
      </strong>
      Only {studiedInMcas} of {treatments.length} entries have been studied in MCAS
      patients at all, and even those are uncontrolled. So every entry states two things
      instead: how directly it has been studied in mast cells, and which conditions it is
      actually approved or trialled for.
    </p>
    <p class="prose-body mt-3" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      The first of those carries a distinction worth knowing before you ask about
      anything: {downstream} of these do not act on mast cells at all. They block or
      degrade a mediator after release. That is a perfectly coherent thing to do — it is
      simply a different kind of intervention from a drug aimed at the cell itself, and
      nothing on this site used to tell you which was which.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2">
      {
        MAST_CELL_BASIS.map((basis: MastCellBasis) => (
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-3 gap-y-1 text-sm">
            <a
              href={href(`/methodology#basis-${basis}`)}
              class:list={['badge', `basis-${basis}`]}
            >
              {MAST_CELL_BASIS_INFO[basis].label}
            </a>
            <span style="color: var(--text-muted);">{MAST_CELL_BASIS_INFO[basis].definition}</span>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl font-semibold">What the site refuses to do</h2>
    <dl class="prose-body mt-3 space-y-4" style="color: var(--text-muted);">
      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold" style="color: var(--text);">
          Average away a disagreement
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Published food lists contradict each other more than patients are usually told,
          and the sharpest conflicts are between a list and a laboratory: 60% of reviewed
          low-histamine diets exclude citrus, which contains no detectable histamine.
          Ratings are stored and shown per source so those conflicts stay visible instead
          of being averaged into one confident-looking number.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold" style="color: var(--text);">
          Let borrowed evidence pass as its own
        </dt>
        <dd>
          MCAS relapses and remits, so improvement after starting something is not
          evidence that the something caused it. Every entry must state in plain words
          what its evidence cannot establish, and the schema rejects any that omits it.
          Listing a condition as approved requires citing the current label; listing one
          as trialled requires citing the published trial. A registered protocol or a
          study stopped after two participants is a fact about a trial, not a result
          from one.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold" style="color: var(--text);">
          Trust an aggregator for a trial status
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Trial statuses come from ClinicalTrials.gov or the sponsor, never from a
          tracker site — the data model has no field value for one. Aggregators cache the
          last confident-sounding status and show it long after the underlying record has
          gone quiet.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="font-semibold" style="color: var(--text);">Go stale quietly</dt>
        <dd>
          All {citationCount} citations are re-checked weekly by a scheduled job, and every
          entry displays the date a human last verified it. Entries that fall out of date
          say so on their own faces.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</Base>
